---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { listClaimStats } from '../lib/claims';
import { LEIDEN, LICENCE, SITE } from '../lib/site';

/** Read from the export rather than typed into the sentence, so the copy cannot come to
 *  disagree with `SORTABLE_MINIMUM` in scripts/export.mjs. Ten when the export has told us
 *  nothing yet. */
const claimSortMinimum = [...listClaimStats().values()][0]?.sortableMinimum ?? 10;
---

<Page
  title="About"
  description="What MathemAct is for, why accounts follow a fixed schema, and how verification works."
  lead="MathemAct is a place for mathematicians to document, examine, and shape how AI is becoming part of mathematical practice."
>
  <h2>Why MathemAct</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools are already being used in mathematical research, learning, teaching, and writing. But most of what
    we learn from using them disappears: what worked, what failed, what required careful checking, and what changed
    the way we approached a problem.
  </p>

  <p>
    MathemAct exists to make that experience visible.
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal is not to decide whether AI is good or bad for mathematics. It is to build a public record
    of how these tools are actually being used, so that mathematicians can learn from one another and make
    informed decisions about how mathematical practice should evolve.
  </p>

  <h2>A record of practice</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct is built around first-hand accounts rather than general impressions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Contributions follow a common structure: what you were trying to do, which tool you used, what happened,
    and how you checked that the result was correct.
  </p>

  <p>
    Failures belong here as much as successes. So do partial successes, unexpected uses, and approaches
    that stopped working when models changed.
  </p>

  <p>
    The structure is deliberate. It makes individual experiences easier to compare, search, revisit, and
    eventually study as a collective record.
  </p>

  <h2>A space to learn, question, and build</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct has three parts:
    <ul>
      <li><b>Educate</b> collects concrete reports and experiences from which we can all learn.</li>
      <li><b>Agitate</b> is a place to question existing habits and propose alternative ones.</li>
      <li><b>Organize</b> gathers the tools, entries, projects, and people needed to turn those ideas into practice.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    The three names are a reference to a quote by Antonio Gramsci. The point here is that the changes
    brought by AI should not merely happen to mathematical practice; mathematicians should be equipped to shape
    them collectively.
  </p>

  <h2>A respectful community</h2>

    <p>
    We encourage users to adhere to the website's <a href={path('/code-of-conduct/')}>code of conduct</a>.
    In it, we have attempted to establish ground rules for what constitutes acceptable vs. unacceptable behavior.
    The document also details what happens to users who violate its principles.
    </p>

    <p>
    MathemAct can count on a couple of volunteer moderators. They are not gatekeepers for content:
    anything users post is published right away, withouth needing a review. However, moderation exists
    as a tool to make sure that the content of the website remains appopriate and respectful: 
    users have the possibility to flag content if they deem it inappropriate, a moderator then
    decides whether it stays up and writes an explanation that both the author and the flagger read.
    For serious and repeated violations, accounts can be suspended. Suspended users can still access the
    site, but in read-only mode, they can't post anything. The content they have already posted stays
    up, but each contribution can be flagged and reviewed as any other post and comment can. Suspended
    users can appeal the decision and a second moderator will review it.
    </p>

    <p>
    Full details on how moderation, flagging and supensions work can be found on the
    <a href={path('/code-of-conduct/')}>code of conduct</a> page.
    </p>

  <h2>A place of openness and trust</h2>

  <p>
    Contributions can be made under a real name or a pseudonym. Institutional verification is available for
    people who want to establish that they are connected to a research institution without revealing their
    email address.
  </p>

  <p>
    Published contributions are openly available under a <a href={LICENCE.url} rel="license"
      >{LICENCE.fullName} ({LICENCE.name})</a
    >, and the underlying corpus is made available as structured data.
  </p> 

  <p>
    While MathemAct was not inspired by the <a class="principles__link" href={LEIDEN.url}>Leiden Declaration on Artificial Intelligence and Mathematics</a>, its aims
    closely align with the declaration’s call for transparent disclosure of AI and computational tools in
    mathematical work.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>MathemAct is an independent project and is not affiliated with any existing group or institution.</b>
  </p>

  <h2>An experiment</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct is itself experimental and the code is <a href={SITE.repository}>public</a>. Its structure,
    categories, and ways of presenting information will evolve as people use it and as mathematical practice changes. 
  </p>

  <p>
    Suggestions, criticism, complaints, and requests for new features are highly appreciated. You can
    send them to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Reports of things that do not work are especially useful. If you run into technical issues, please reach out to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>
    detailing what went wrong. The website is actively maintained, even though on a volunteer basis: we will address
    your concern at the earliest opportunity.
  </p>

  <h2>Writing mathematics</h2>

  <p>
    Accounts are written in Markdown, and TeX between dollar signs is rendered when the
    page is built. Nothing is sent to a third-party service to typeset it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is behind it</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct was started by <b>Anna Zanoli</b>, a PhD student in number theory at IST Austria.
  </p>

  <p>The website is built and maintained together with her sister Emma.</p>
</Page>

<style>

  .tiers {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .tiers dt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .tiers dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }

  .legend {
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Inline flow, not flex — see the note in index.astro. A flex li turns the trailing
     description into a single unbreakable item that wraps to its own line. */
  .legend > li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
